.TH byobu-enable-shell-integration 1 "15 August 2026" byobu "byobu"
.SH NAME
byobu-enable-shell-integration, byobu-disable-shell-integration \- add and remove OSC 133 semantic-prompt markers in your shell configuration

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbyobu-enable-shell-integration\fP
.TP
\fBbyobu-disable-shell-integration\fP

.SH DESCRIPTION

Byobu can emit OSC 133 semantic-prompt markers: escape sequences marking where a prompt starts, where a prompt ends and command input begins, where a command's output starts, and where a command finishes (with its exit code). This is the same protocol iTerm2, Kitty, Ghostty, VS Code, and Warp already use for jump-to-previous-prompt navigation and command-output selection; any terminal or tool that understands OSC 133 can locate prompts and command boundaries precisely instead of guessing from rendered text.

This is off by default. \fBbyobu-enable-shell-integration\fP adds one line to \fI~/.bashrc\fP (for bash) or \fI~/.zshrc\fP (for zsh), sourcing the marker-emitting logic. It layers onto whatever prompt you already have \(em byobu's own colorized prompt from \fBbyobu-enable-prompt\fP(1), a framework like oh-my-zsh or starship, or a plain unconfigured prompt \(em rather than replacing it.

You can safely remove the line from your shell configuration file yourself; it ends in \fB#byobu-shell-integration#\fP.

\fBbyobu-disable-shell-integration\fP removes that line.

Only bash and zsh are supported. Running \fBbyobu-enable-shell-integration\fP under any other shell prints an error and does nothing.

.SH SEE ALSO
.PD 0
